    11	## Orchestration stop rules
    12	
    13	These rules implement the user's September 8 request to prevent repeated orchestration displacement.
    14	The lead agent owns their application. Record decisions in the existing task record.
    15	Do not create a new approval system, dashboard or harness for these rules.
    16	
    17	1. **Name the capability before the next action.** State what the developer will be able to do.
    18	   Name the command or observable result that demonstrates it.
    19	   A packet, hash manifest, allocation, review dispatch or checkpoint is supporting work.
    20	   None independently counts as a delivered capability.
    21	   When the user explicitly requests documentation, the requested document is the deliverable.
    22	
    23	2. **Stop repeated failure of the same approach.** Two failed implementation/result-review cycles with the same defect class trigger this rule.
    24	   Do not dispatch a third broad correction of that approach.
    25	   First reproduce one defect through the public API or smallest executable mechanism.
    26	   Change the implementation strategy or task size, and demonstrate why the change addresses the defect.
    27	   Apply the reproducer and a bounded repair under existing authority.
    28	   Retain the original acceptance requirements.
    29	   Rewording a packet, changing an identifier or increasing a timeout does not reset this trigger.
    30	
    31	3. **Interrupt process-only work.** Two consecutive orchestration cycles without implementation, a decisive experiment or a resolved concrete blocker trigger this rule.
    32	   Thirty minutes spent only on orchestration administration also triggers it.
    33	   An orchestration cycle means preparing, dispatching, receiving and dispositioning one task or review.
    34	   A resolved blocker must identify the previously failing operation now enabled or a decisive technical finding.
    35	   A new packet approval or resource allocation alone does not reset this trigger.
    36	   A genuinely running required build, proof or test is not administration time.
    37	   Stop adding packets and bookkeeping work.
    38	   Inspect or test the production path, repair the current defect, or continue an independent eligible implementation task.
    39	   If none is possible, state the concrete blocker and evidence without inventing another preparatory dependency.
    40	
    41	4. **Inspect the production path before reporting candidate success.** Trace one input from the public entry point to its observable result.
    42	   Reject an unconditional throw, constant admission response or unused adapter where execution is required.
    43	   Source-only admission restricts invocation, not implementation of the approved production behavior.
    44	   Test operation order and input-dependent output through that path using controlled transport when necessary.
    45	   Report missing live tests or real transaction fixtures separately.
    46	   Never relabel synthetic transport results as network evidence.
    47	
    48	5. **Make tests challenge behavior.** Derive at least one relevant adversarial input independently from the acceptance requirement.
    49	   Check complete material state when the claim requires complete state.
    50	   Reject decisions derived from mutation names, expected errors or copied expected outputs.
    51	   A regression must exercise the defect through the callable mechanism.
    52	   Test counts, stable generation and hashes cannot replace that check.
    53	
    54	6. **Separate repairs from consequential decisions.** Repair approved behavior without a new proposal when scope and authorized limits remain unchanged.
    55	   Record only the changed requirement, evidence and resource delta in the existing task record.
    56	   Use the user-required majority process for consequential design or resource changes.
    57	   Justify renewed resources with a changed hypothesis or decisive remaining test, not sunk cost or unfinished status.
    58	   Do not exceed an existing limit or waive a failed gate to avoid review.
    59	   A genuine limit stops that run, but does not automatically stop independent eligible work.
    60	
    61	7. **Own integration and limit unfinished work.** Keep one primary capability in focus.
    62	   Delegate independent work only when it has a clear boundary and available review capacity.
    63	   Do not create another dependent candidate while its recurring prerequisite defect lacks a reproducer and changed approach.
    64	   Preserve all sprint requirements and dependency gates.
    65	   Work on permitted provisional components without claiming a semantic freeze or broader acceptance.

